commit 9743f1f4f84bfa082bf166b98bffb948c3eee70d
Author: Ice Casasola <iscasasolaii@gmail.com>
Date:   Sun Jul 26 16:25:01 2026 +0800

    fix(security): SEC-4b repair — close the DELETE lane, un-vacuum the guards
    
    Repairs the adversarial findings against #3738. Three of them were the same
    class of defect the PR exists to prevent: a control that reads as rigorous and
    enforces nothing.
    
    THE GUARDS WERE VACUOUS (proven by mutation, both before and after):
    
      • lib/order-price-authority.test.ts leaked a /g regex lastIndex, dropping the
        FIRST orders-insert in every file — i.e. every insert in the nine
        single-mint files. It then sliced the first `{` after `.insert(`, which the
        orderRowFor(identity, fields) refactor had turned into the IDENTITY object.
        Real coverage: 2 payloads in 1 file the PR never touched, out of 16 insert
        sites. Reintroducing the literal SEC-4 bug (a browser-supplied
        requested_total_php) plus a formData-supplied user_id left the suite green.
        Now both mutations go red.
      • The same scan only covered app/ + lib/, missing apps/web/scripts/ — which
        already contains an orders+payments minter. Widened to apps/web + packages/
        + scripts/; the hidden minter is now on the reviewed roster explicitly.
      • Added an ANTI-VACUITY test that asserts the DENOMINATOR: every orders
        insert must resolve to readable source and name its amount column. A source
        scanner that stops matching does not go red, it goes quiet — this makes it
        go red. (It immediately caught a multi-line type annotation in checkout
        blinding the resolver.)
    
      • tests/db/orders-payments-insert-revoke.db.test.ts re-executed the migration
        file after the replay, which also re-created whatever a LATER migration had
        dropped. A regression migration that re-granted INSERT and dropped both
        guard triggers left it 13/13 green. replay-migrations.ts now takes
        `preserveRevoked`: it seeds the CREATE-TABLE-time default ACL when the table
        first appears, lets migrations run against it, and undoes only the harness's
        own blanket grant. A re-grant survives into the test. 11/18 now fail against
        that regression migration, and three REGRESSION tests name the cause.
    
        (My first attempt at this snapshotted post-replay state, which cannot tell
        "a migration revoked it" from "PGlite never granted it" — it reported the
        table as locked down with the fix NEUTRALISED. Caught by running the
        neutralisation experiment rather than trusting the design.)
    
    THE DELETE LANE WAS LEFT OPEN. orders_owner_write is FOR ALL with no restrictive
    DELETE counterpart, so a buyer could DELETE their own PAID order: payments,
    receipts, order_ledger and vendor_payouts CASCADE, while the entitlement tables
    are SET NULL. They keep the SKU and erase the payment record, the BIR receipt
    and the audit trail. Revoked, and the caller guard now fires BEFORE INSERT OR
    DELETE. Safe because every app-side rollback DELETE moved to service_role.
    
    THE GUARD HAD AN is_admin() ESCAPE, in the one scenario it exists for. is_admin()
    is satisfied by a session-role admin JWT, not only by service_role — so after a
    stray re-GRANT any admin account regained ₱1 minting. Removed; the deny-list on
    ('authenticated','anon') is what keeps migrations and psql working. Tested.
    
    CROSS-TENANT HOLE (the headline exploit's remaining route). The four
    vendor_*__<id> families encode a vendor-owned target in the service_key and
    their activation hooks provision straight off that string. None has a catalog
    row, so resolveServiceSellability returns 'unknown' → ALLOW → couple checkout
    kept the browser's price: vendor_additional_branch__<a stranger's branch_id> for
    ₱1, paid for real, activating someone else's subscription on approval. Rejected
    at checkout (gate 1) and re-checked at activation against the order's own
    vendor_profile_id (gate 2, load-bearing — holds for any origin). A drift test
    re-derives the prefix list from the hooks so a fifth family cannot be added
    without one.
    
    THE RETIRED STATUS POLICIES. orders_insert_status_guard and
    payments_insert_status_guard both short-circuit on auth.role()='service_role',
    so they stopped constraining anything at the exact moment user_id stopped being
    constrained. Re-imposed in order-mint-identity: payments pin status:'pending'
    and forbid the caller setting it; orders enforce the mintable allow-list with a
    requested_total_php===0 escape for the free-cycle audit rows. A PRICED order can
    no longer be minted already-'paid'.
    
    Also: branches/actions.ts moved off the global-highest resolveVendorRole (the
    one converted money site still using it, which its own new comment claimed was
    adequate); createMoneyWriterClient() so money writes name the missing
    SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY instead of silently degrading to the anon key in dev
    and failing as an opaque 42501; and the migration header no longer implies SEC-4
    is fully closed — SEC-7 (catalog-less SKUs keep the browser's price through the
    sanctioned action) is called out explicitly.
    
    tsc clean · unit 3880/3880 · db 279/279 · lint clean (warnings pre-existing).
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

